1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,335
Stir your coffee as much as you like.

2
00:00:03,455 --> 00:00:07,777
Swirl it one way, then the other. Just stir smoothly, without splashing.

3
00:00:07,897 --> 00:00:12,554
When it settles, at least one point on the surface is exactly where it started.

4
00:00:12,674 --> 00:00:14,865
That's not luck. It's a theorem.

5
00:00:19,565 --> 00:00:22,395
Here's another picture. Take a map of a park.

6
00:00:22,515 --> 00:00:25,912
Lay it on the ground, anywhere inside that same park.

7
00:00:26,032 --> 00:00:30,765
Rotate it, shrink it, even crumple it, as long as it stays inside the park.

8
00:00:30,885 --> 00:00:35,514
Then some point on the map lies exactly on top of the real place it shows.

9
00:00:35,634 --> 00:00:42,663
For a simple rotate-and-shrink, you can even find it: keep applying the same move, and the maps spiral right into it.

10
00:00:44,663 --> 00:00:52,037
This is Brouwer's fixed point theorem, proved by the Dutch mathematician L. E. J. Brouwer around 1910.

11
00:00:52,157 --> 00:00:56,094
Let D be a closed disk, or a solid ball in any dimension.

12
00:00:56,214 --> 00:01:04,300
If f is a continuous map from D into itself, then f has a fixed point: a point x with f(x) = x.

13
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:10,629
Give every point an arrow, from where it is to where it goes. Somewhere, an arrow must have length zero.

14
00:01:12,229 --> 00:01:14,986
In one dimension, you already know this theorem.

15
00:01:15,106 --> 00:01:21,512
The disk becomes the interval from 0 to 1, and the graph of f is a continuous curve in the unit square.

16
00:01:21,632 --> 00:01:25,503
It starts on or above the diagonal, and ends on or below it.

17
00:01:25,623 --> 00:01:33,026
So it must cross the line y = x. The crossing is a fixed point. That's just the intermediate value theorem.

18
00:01:34,226 --> 00:01:35,979
Every condition matters.

19
00:01:36,099 --> 00:01:40,165
Cut a hole in the disk, and a simple rotation moves every point.

20
00:01:40,285 --> 00:01:44,480
Remove the boundary, and points can slide toward an edge that isn't there.

21
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:48,204
Allow a jump, and the curve can leap right over the diagonal.

22
00:01:48,324 --> 00:01:53,815
The space must be closed, bounded and without holes, and the map must be continuous.

23
00:01:55,215 --> 00:02:01,171
So why is it true? One of the most beautiful proofs is a coloring puzzle, called Sperner's lemma.

24
00:02:01,291 --> 00:02:07,131
Take a big triangle with a red, a green and a blue corner, and cut it into small triangles.

25
00:02:07,251 --> 00:02:14,994
Now color every vertex. The only rule: a vertex on a side must use one of the two colors at the ends of that side.

26
00:02:15,114 --> 00:02:16,879
Inside, anything goes.

27
00:02:16,999 --> 00:02:24,248
Sperner's lemma says there is always a small triangle with all three colors. In fact, there's an odd number of them.

28
00:02:24,368 --> 00:02:30,112
Here's the proof. Think of each small triangle as a room, and each red-green edge as a door.

29
00:02:30,232 --> 00:02:38,327
Along the bottom side, the colors go from red to green, so there's an odd number of doors. The other two sides have no doors at all.

30
00:02:38,447 --> 00:02:46,438
Walk in through a door, and leave each room by its other door. A room has at most two doors, so you never enter the same room twice.

31
00:02:46,558 --> 00:02:53,531
So the walk either leaves through another door on the bottom, or it stops in a room with just one door: a rainbow room.

32
00:02:53,651 --> 00:03:00,250
The bottom doors pair up, but there's an odd number of them. So at least one walk must end in a rainbow room.

33
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:07,634
Now, back to Brouwer. Let f map the triangle into itself, and draw the arrows again.

34
00:03:07,754 --> 00:03:11,484
Color each vertex by a corner its arrow moves away from.

35
00:03:11,604 --> 00:03:17,891
A point on a side can only move away from that side's two corners, so this is a legal Sperner coloring.

36
00:03:18,011 --> 00:03:24,012
So there's a rainbow triangle: a tiny spot where the arrows move away from all three corners.

37
00:03:24,132 --> 00:03:29,222
Make the grid finer and finer, and the rainbow triangles close in on a single point.

38
00:03:29,342 --> 00:03:33,380
There, the arrow would have to move away from all three corners at once.

39
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:39,510
But the three coordinates always add up to one, so they can't all go down. None of them can change.

40
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:42,602
The arrow has length zero. That's the fixed point.

41
00:03:44,602 --> 00:03:47,216
This idea reaches far beyond geometry.

42
00:03:47,336 --> 00:03:56,200
In 1950, John Nash used a fixed point theorem to prove that every finite game has an equilibrium, if players may randomize.

43
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:58,919
That work later earned him a Nobel Prize.

44
00:03:59,039 --> 00:04:08,612
In the board game Hex, a full board always has exactly one winner. Amazingly, that fact is equivalent to Brouwer's theorem in two dimensions.

45
00:04:08,732 --> 00:04:14,833
And Sperner's lemma can even tell roommates how to split the rent, so that nobody wants to swap rooms.

46
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:20,432
But notice what the theorem doesn't tell you: where the fixed point is.

47
00:04:20,552 --> 00:04:22,419
It's a pure existence proof.

48
00:04:22,539 --> 00:04:26,910
Ironically, Brouwer later became the great champion of intuitionism,

49
00:04:27,030 --> 00:04:31,990
a school which insists that to prove something exists, you must show how to construct it.

50
00:04:32,110 --> 00:04:37,844
Today, how hard it is to actually find a fixed point is a deep question in computer science.

51
00:04:37,964 --> 00:04:43,744
So the next time you stir your coffee, remember: somewhere in that cup, one point hasn't moved at all.
